Resolution from the plan owner: option 1 approved — fix `test/profile-events-section.test.tsx`'s `findAllByType` helper to resolve function-component boundaries, rather than leaving `EventsSection` as JSX-that-the-test-can't-see-into or switching to a function-call workaround.

Root cause confirmed: `findAllByType` only recurses via `el.props.children`. `<EventsSection events={...} emptyTitle={...} .../>` has no `children` prop at all (its data comes in via other named props), so the walk dead-ends there regardless of what's inside. The fix is to make the walker invoke unmatched function-component elements to resolve what they'd render, then continue the walk into that.

Apply this exact replacement for `findAllByType` in `test/profile-events-section.test.tsx` (replaces the current implementation at lines 54-70, same file, only this function changes — nothing else in the test file changes):

```ts
// Recursively collect every element of a given component type in the tree.
// Resolves function-component boundaries (invokes them with their own
// props) so the walk can see past a wrapper like EventsSection into what
// it renders — otherwise a component whose data comes in via non-children
// props (not the `children` prop) hides everything inside it from a walk
// that only recurses through `.props.children`.
function findAllByType(node: unknown, type: unknown): ReactElement[] {
  const acc: ReactElement[] = [];
  const visit = (n: unknown) => {
    if (!n || typeof n !== "object") return;
    if (Array.isArray(n)) {
      n.forEach(visit);
      return;
    }
    const el = n as ReactElement;
    if (el.type === type) {
      acc.push(el);
    } else if (typeof el.type === "function") {
      visit((el.type as (props: unknown) => unknown)(el.props));
      return;
    }
    const children = (el.props as { children?: unknown } | undefined)?.children;
    if (children) visit(children);
  };
  visit(node);
  return acc;
}
```

Note the branch structure: a matched element (`el.type === type`) still falls through to the children check afterward (preserves original behavior exactly for that case — don't invoke List/NoEventsFound themselves, since the test needs to inspect their `.props`, not what they render). Only an unmatched function-component type gets invoked-and-recursed-into, with an early `return` so it doesn't also hit the trailing children check.

Do this:
1. Apply that replacement to `test/profile-events-section.test.tsx`.
2. Re-run `yarn vitest run test/profile-events-section.test.tsx` — expect 3/3 passing again.
3. Re-run `yarn typecheck`.
4. Commit everything (EventsSection.tsx, ProfileEventsSection.tsx, and the test file fix) in one commit. Use this commit message instead of the brief's original (since it now includes the test fix):

```
